#statement("Definition", title: [Galilean group])[
The #strong[Galilean group]#index-entry("Galilean group") is the group of #strong[Galilean transformations] generated by rotations in $bb(R)^n$, translations in $bb(R)^(n + 1)$ and #strong[Galilean boosts] $\(x\,t\)mapsto\(x + t v\,t\)$ #display-equation($mat(delim: "(", R, v, w; 0, 1, s; 0, 0, 1) vec(x, t, 1) = vec(R x + t v + w, t + s, 1)$)

]
#statement("Definition", title: [Lorentz group])[
The #strong[Lorentz group]#index-entry("Lorentz group") is the group of #strong[Lorentz transformations] generated by rotations in $bb(R)^n$ and #strong[Lorentz boosts]#index-entry("Lorentz boost") $\(x\,t\)mapsto (sinh s x - cosh s t \, sinh s t - cosh s x)$

]
#statement("Definition", title: [Poincaré group])[
The #strong[Galilean group]#index-entry("Galilean group") is the isometry group of the Minkowski space $bb(R)^(n + 1)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$\(x\,c t\)mapsto (gamma (x - v t) \, gamma (t - frac(v x, c^2)))$ $beta = v / c$, $alpha = sqrt(1 - beta^2)$. $gamma = 1 / sqrt(1 - beta^2)$ is the #strong[Lorentz factor]#index-entry("Lorentz factor") $cases(t' = gamma (t - frac(v x, c^2)), x' = gamma (x - v t))$, where $\(x\,t\)$ and $\(x'\,t'\)$ are the coordinates of two frames, and frame $\(x'\,t'\)$ is moving towards the positive direction of the $x$ axis with velocity $v$, and $c$ is the speed of light, we can find the inverse transformation $cases(t = gamma (t' + frac(v x', c^2)), x = gamma (x' + v t'))$ which makes perfect sense since relatively speaking, frame $\(x\,t\)$ is moving towards the negative direction of the $x'$ axis with velocity $v$ or rather moving towards the positive direction of the $x'$ axis with velocity $- v$

