Things happen simultaneously in one frame may not be simultaneous in another frame

#statement("Deduction", title: [Length contraction])[
Suppose a train is moving with speed $v$, shed a beam light from one end to get to the other end

$A$ in frame $\(x\,t\)$ send a signal when the left end of train passes, $B$ in frame $\(x'\,t'\)$ on the right end of the train receives and return the signal, suppose the length of the train is $l'$, and the length appears to be $l$ in frame $\(x\,t\)$, then it takes time $l' / c$ for $B$ to receive the signal in $\(x'\,t'\)$, which takes time $frac(l' gamma, c)$ in $\(x\,t\)$, when $B$ should be in distance $l + frac(v l' gamma, c)$ from $A$ in $\(x\,t\)$ but distance $l' + frac(v l', c)$ in $\(x'\,t'\)$ which take time $frac(l + frac(v l' gamma, c), c)$ and $frac(l' + frac(v l', c), c)$ to get back to $A$ in $\(x\,t\)$ and $\(x'\,t'\)$, hence we should have $frac(l + frac(v l' gamma, c), c) = frac(l' + frac(v l', c), c) gamma arrow.r.double l = gamma l'$

]
